{% comment %}
A "template" block cannot nest inside another one — TRMNL's TemplateTag just scans for the
first literal closing token and treats everything before it as the block's body, with no
awareness of nesting, so a template defined inside "main"'s own body would prematurely close
"main" at its own closing token instead. These are top-level siblings of "main", rendered from
inside its body instead (see badge_content/badge_image_row/chip_body's own call sites further
down). NOTE: this comment deliberately never spells out the actual curly-brace-percent tag
delimiters for "template"/"endtemplate"/"render" as literal text, even in prose, even inside
this very comment block — Liquid's tokenizer splits the WHOLE raw file on those delimiters
before any tag's own semantics run (comment or not), so writing them out here would itself get
parsed as a real, malformed tag and corrupt everything after it — which is exactly the bug that
broke this file's rendering entirely the first time this comment (and two similarly-worded ones
elsewhere below) were written the obvious way, with the delimiters spelled out.
{% endcomment %}

{% template badge_content %}
  {%- comment -%} One badge's inner content — icon (inverted line-art) or a plain letter —
  identical wherever it's hosted (the rail, or "image" display mode's whole-chip row). Param:
  b. Photos are DELIBERATELY not rendered here for now: a real photo dithers to muddy noise at
  badge size on TRMNL's actual e-ink output (confirmed against a real device render, not just
  a browser preview — browsers never applied that dithering, so it looked fine there right up
  until it didn't on the device) regardless of how big the badge itself is; fixing that for
  real needs either pre-processing uploaded photos for e-ink legibility (posterize/contrast
  before TRMNL's own dither ever sees them) or steering people toward a flat/cartoon image
  (already suggested elsewhere — see imagetocartoon.com in CONFIG.md/the editor's Photo field)
  — either is a bigger change than this. A person with `image` set falls back to their badge
  letter (b.person's own first initial) instead of a blank box in the meantime; kind is still
  "photo" upstream (transform.js/run()) so this reverts cleanly once photos are worth showing
  again. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if b.kind == "icon" -%}
    <img src="{{ b.src }}" alt="" style="width:65%;height:65%;filter:invert(1);">
  {%- elsif b.kind == "photo" -%}
    {{ b.person | slice: 0, 1 | upcase }}
  {%- else -%}
    {{ b.text }}
  {%- endif -%}
{% endtemplate %}

{% template badge_image_row %}
  {%- comment -%} "image" display mode (see categories[].display in transform.js): badge(s)
  fill the whole chip, centered in a row — same markup for all-day and timed events. Params:
  badges, image_d. {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="flex flex--row flex--center-x flex--center-y w--full h--full" style="gap:4px;">
    {%- for b in badges -%}
      <span class="rounded--small flex flex--center-x flex--center-y text--white bg--black" style="width:{{ image_d }}px;height:{{ image_d }}px;flex-shrink:0;overflow:hidden;border:2px solid currentColor;box-sizing:border-box;">
        {%- render "badge_content", b: b -%}
      </span>
    {%- endfor -%}
  </div>
{% endtemplate %}

{% template chip_body %}
  {%- comment -%} The left "meaning" rail (plain color accent, or 1-2 badge circles) plus the
  title — every all-day and timed-event chip renders this SAME template instead of each
  carrying its own copy. Badges sit SIDE BY SIDE (flex--row), not stacked — a stacked pair used
  to force each circle down to half the available height, so a solo badge (the common case) and
  a paired one ended up different, smaller-than-necessary sizes; laid out side by side, one
  shared badge_d (see "main") fills the rail's own height regardless of count, and the rail
  itself grows WIDER to fit however many circles it's actually hosting instead of taller. Flush
  to the chip's own left/top/bottom edges — radius is passed in rather than computed here since
  only the all-day caller has a real "which corner is mid-span" question to answer
  (continues_before/after); a timed event always passes the same constant (see
  timed_rail_radius in "main"). clip/fit_min stay real, separate params rather than being
  forced identical: all-day rows contribute real height to a shared flex budget (see that
  chip's own h--[Ncqh] comment), so an oversized title there needs cal-fit-clip to keep it from
  growing the row; a timed event is an absolutely-positioned overlay that can safely let fit()
  leave it visible-overflow instead.
  Params: badges, bar, title, fg, title_cls, fit_min, clip, pad_right, radius, bar_w, badge_d.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if badges.size > 0 -%}
    {%- comment -%} 1px between the two circles themselves (tight — they're one group, "whose
    + what kind"), but generous padding around that group inside the rail (4px) and before the
    title (12px, vs the bare 8px bar_pad below) — the group should read as a unit sitting with
    real breathing room inside the chip, not two separate dots crowding its edges. {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign chip_rail_w = badge_d | times: badges.size | plus: 1 | plus: 8 -%}
    <div class="bg--{{ bar }} flex flex--row flex--center-x flex--center-y" style="position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;left:0;width:{{ chip_rail_w }}px;overflow:hidden;border-radius:{{ radius }};">
      {%- for b in badges -%}
        <span class="rounded--small flex flex--center-x flex--center-y text--white bg--black" style="width:{{ badge_d }}px;height:{{ badge_d }}px;flex-shrink:0;{% unless forloop.first %}margin-left:1px;{% endunless %}overflow:hidden;border:1.5px solid currentColor;box-sizing:border-box;">
          {%- render "badge_content", b: b -%}
        </span>
      {%- endfor -%}
    </div>
    {%- assign chip_pad_left = chip_rail_w | plus: 12 -%}
  {%- else -%}
    <div class="bg--{{ bar }}" style="position:absolute;top:0;bottom:0;left:0;width:{{ bar_w }}px;border-radius:{{ radius }};"></div>
    {%- assign chip_pad_left = bar_w | plus: 8 -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  <span class="title {{ title_cls }} text--{{ fg }} cal-fit-text{% if clip %} cal-fit-clip{% endif %}" data-fit-min="{{ fit_min }}" data-fit-max="90" style="min-width:0;padding-left:{{ chip_pad_left }}px;padding-right:{{ pad_right }}px;">{{ title }}</span>
{% endtemplate %}

{% template chip %}
  {%- comment -%}
  The WHOLE event/all-day chip box — fill, border, radius, and (via chip_body/badge_image_row)
  its content — one render call for both chip types instead of two hand-kept-in-sync
  copies of the outer <div> too (chip_body alone still left that outer box duplicated, which is
  exactly how bar: ended up missing for all-day chips only — two copies of "the same" code
  drift). What's left as real parameters is only what's structurally forced to differ, never
  just historical accident:
    - size_class/size_style: HOW the box gets its size. An all-day chip is a normal-flow row
      that contributes real height to a shared budget (see allday_row_pct), so it needs an
      explicit h--[Ncqh] class + inline height/flex-shrink; a timed event is sized entirely by
      its own absolutely-positioned ancestor (top_pct/height_pct in shared.liquid's day-column
      loop), so it just fills that with h--full/w--full and nothing else.
    - flex_dir: flex--row vs flex--col. Purely cosmetic-internal (the framework's
      flex--center-y/-x classes are direction-aware, so either produces the same visual
      centering here) — kept as whatever each caller already had rather than risking an
      unrelated layout shift for zero visible benefit.
    - pad_cls: top/bottom breathing room. All-day rows are short and fixed-height (py--[1px]);
      a timed event has real slack to spend and uses its own view tier's pt/pb
      (event_pad_cls in "main").
    - box_radius/border_left/border_right/margin_left/margin_right: only an all-day chip can be
      one fragment of a multi-day banner (continues_before/after) needing a squared-off
      mid-span edge and no outline there; a timed event never spans across day columns, so it
      always passes the full radius and every border side.
  Params: hue, fg, bar, title, badges, display, flex_dir, size_class, size_style, pad_cls,
  box_radius, border_left, border_right, margin_left, margin_right, title_cls, fit_min, clip,
  pad_right, rail_radius, bar_w, badge_d, image_d.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="{{ size_class }} bg--{{ hue }} text--{{ fg }} w--full {{ pad_cls }} flex {{ flex_dir }} flex--center-y cal-fit-box" style="position:relative;overflow:hidden;box-sizing:border-box;{{ size_style }}border-radius:{{ box_radius }};border-top:2px solid currentColor;border-bottom:2px solid currentColor;{% if border_left %}border-left:2px solid currentColor;{% endif %}{% if border_right %}border-right:2px solid currentColor;{% endif %}{% if margin_left %}margin-left:-4px;{% endif %}{% if margin_right %}margin-right:-4px;{% endif %}">
    {%- if display == "image" -%}
      {%- comment -%} "image" display mode (see categories[].display in transform.js): the
      badge rail is skipped entirely — the badge(s) become the chip's whole content instead of
      a strip down the side. Only ever set once at least one real badge resolved (see run()'s
      badge assembly in transform.js), so this never renders an empty chip. {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- render "badge_image_row", badges: badges, image_d: image_d -%}
    {%- else -%}
      {%- render "chip_body", badges: badges, bar: bar, title: title, fg: fg, title_cls: title_cls, fit_min: fit_min, clip: clip, pad_right: pad_right, radius: rail_radius, bar_w: bar_w, badge_d: badge_d -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
{% endtemplate %}

{% template main %}
<style>
/* The framework's static title/label classes cap out at --xxlarge (40px/30px) — no bigger
   tier exists to reach for on the full view's day label when it's already sitting at that
   ceiling in landscape. Rotated to portrait, the same "OG" 800x480 device has much taller
   rows to spend, so bump it further with a plain override scoped to the real screen--lg +
   screen--portrait combo the framework's own compiled CSS uses (confirmed via its generated
   selectors, e.g. .screen--lg.screen--portrait .lg\:portrait\:title--xxlarge). */
.trmnl .screen--lg.screen--portrait .cal-full-daylabel { font-size: 44px; }
</style>
{%- if view == 4 -%}
  {%- comment -%} quadrant: a quarter of the full-view screen area — needs its own,
  smaller floor rather than sharing full's or even the half-views' sizes.

  event_title_cls: event titles previously had NO static size class at all — just the bare
  "title" class (26px) plus cal-fit-text's JS-driven inline font-size to shrink it down per
  box. Every other piece of text on this page (day label, axis, temps) has a properly tiered
  static class as its baseline; events didn't, so if the JS sizing script doesn't run (or
  doesn't run in time) on whatever renders the real device's screenshot, the title is stuck at
  the full 26px default — overflowing/clipping badly against these views' much smaller event
  boxes regardless of what the JS would have picked. Giving it a sane tiered starting size
  fixes the worst case outright; the JS still fine-tunes per-box on top of it when it runs.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign head_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
  {%- assign chip_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
  {%- assign axis_cls = "label--small" -%}
  {%- assign event_pad_cls = "pt--0 pb--1" -%}
  {%- assign event_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
  {%- assign pad_right = 4 -%}
  {%- assign bar_w = 3 -%}
  {%- assign badge_d = 20 -%}
  {%- assign head_badge_d = 16 -%}
  {%- assign image_d = 24 -%}
{%- elsif view == 3 -%}
  {%- comment -%} half_vertical: only ~400px WIDE (3 narrow day columns, ~133px each) —
  width-constrained rather than area-constrained, so it needs a smaller FONT tier than
  half_horizontal even though both are nominally "half" the full-view area. Day-label font
  matches quadrant's (title--small) rather than getting its own tier — same reasoning as
  quadrant: narrow columns need the compact size regardless of view. image_d (the "image"
  display mode circle) still shares the full view's larger tier below it — that mode has no
  title text competing for room, so half_vertical's narrow columns don't constrain it the way
  they constrain badge_d (see its own comment further down: side-by-side badges spend rail
  WIDTH now, not just height, so badge_d follows this view's narrow-WIDTH font tier instead of
  its full-SCREEN-HEIGHT row budget). {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign head_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
  {%- assign chip_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
  {%- assign axis_cls = "label--base lg:label--large" -%}
  {%- assign event_pad_cls = "pt--0 pb--1" -%}
  {%- assign event_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
  {%- assign pad_right = 4 -%}
  {%- assign bar_w = 3 -%}
  {%- comment -%} badge_d breaks from the "shares full view's tier" reasoning above: that
  applied to the OLD stacked-badge rail, where only row HEIGHT mattered and half_vertical's is
  genuinely full-screen tall. Side-by-side badges (see chip_body) also spend rail WIDTH, and
  half_vertical's ~133px-wide columns can't absorb the full-tier rail without wrapping/crushing
  the title — quadrant's smaller badge_d fits this view's real width constraint instead.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign badge_d = 18 -%}
  {%- comment -%} head_badge_d, unlike badge_d just above, does NOT drop to the narrow-width
  tier here — today's header badges wrap into 2 rows (see the header markup below) rather than
  spending width the way a single-row event-chip rail does, so this view's genuinely tall
  ~480px-screen header still has the room the full-tier size needs; it's the FONT tier
  (head_title_cls) that stays compact for width, not this. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign head_badge_d = 26 -%}
  {%- assign image_d = 42 -%}
{%- elsif view == 2 -%}
  {%- comment -%} half_horizontal: full 800px width but half SCREEN HEIGHT (~240px, same as
  quadrant) — width is ample so the FONT tier can be larger than half_vertical/quadrant, but
  badge sizing shares quadrant's tighter tier below since it's bound by the same short row
  height (allday_row_pct/MIN_EVENT_PCT), not by width. {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign head_title_cls = "title--large lg:title--xlarge" -%}
  {%- assign chip_title_cls = "title--small lg:title--base" -%}
  {%- assign axis_cls = "label--base lg:label--large" -%}
  {%- assign event_pad_cls = "pt--1 pb--2" -%}
  {%- assign event_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
  {%- assign pad_right = 8 -%}
  {%- assign bar_w = 4 -%}
  {%- assign badge_d = 20 -%}
  {%- assign head_badge_d = 16 -%}
  {%- assign image_d = 24 -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- comment -%} full / "OG" 800x480 device: the hour axis at xlarge/xxlarge read too big
  in practice — one tier down still reads fine at this size and leaves more room. Rotated to
  portrait, the same device has much taller rows and narrower columns, so it can (and should)
  read bigger still — lg:portrait: is a real combinator the framework generates (confirmed via
  its compiled CSS, e.g. .screen--lg.screen--portrait .lg\:portrait\:title--xxlarge), so temps
  get one more explicit tier there; the day label is already at the framework's largest static
  tier (title--xxlarge) even without portrait, so it needs a custom size bump instead (see the
  cal-full-daylabel rule below the template). {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign head_title_cls = "title--xlarge lg:title--xxlarge" -%}
  {%- assign chip_title_cls = "title--base lg:title--large lg:portrait:title--xlarge" -%}
  {%- assign axis_cls = "label--large lg:label--xxlarge" -%}
  {%- assign event_pad_cls = "pt--1 pb--2" -%}
  {%- assign event_title_cls = "title--base" -%}
  {%- assign pad_right = 8 -%}
  {%- assign bar_w = 5 -%}
  {%- comment -%} Same badge tier as half_vertical (view 3) — both are full SCREEN HEIGHT
  (~480px), the axis that actually constrains badge sizing (see the badge_pct comments further
  down and view 3's own comment above). {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign badge_d = 38 -%}
  {%- assign head_badge_d = 26 -%}
  {%- assign image_d = 42 -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%} Left "meaning" rail on every event/all-day chip — see the chip_body template
below, rendered identically by both chip types instead of each having its own bespoke
treatment. bar_w: how wide the rail is with NO badges in it — just the plain color accent it's
always been. badge_d: how big each circle is once the rail is actually hosting badges — ONE
size regardless of count, since chip_body lays badges out SIDE BY SIDE rather than stacking
them, so a circle's size is bounded only by the chip's own height (same whether it's a solo
badge or one of a pair) and the rail simply grows WIDER to fit however many there are, instead
of each circle having to shrink to share a fixed rail width. image_d is a separate, larger
circle size for "image" display mode (see categories[].display in transform.js), where the
badge(s) become the WHOLE chip's content instead of a rail down the side. pad_right is the
fixed breathing room on the title's other (non-rail) side; the rail-side padding is computed
inside chip_body itself, from however many badges that chip actually ended up with. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign timed_rail_radius = "7px 0 0 7px" -%}


{%- comment -%} Every tier above assumes the original 3-day layout's column width. With
Days to Show now configurable, the SAME day columns get much narrower at 5 or 7 days —
"Za 4 Juli" fit fine at 3 columns wide but "Zo 5 Juli" wrapped and lost "Juli" off the
bottom of the fixed-height header at 7. Force the day-label down a tier (or two) once
there are more columns than the sizes above were tuned for, regardless of view. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- if days.size >= 6 -%}
  {%- assign head_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
  {%- assign chip_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
{%- elsif days.size >= 4 -%}
  {%- assign head_title_cls = "title--base lg:title--large" -%}
  {%- assign chip_title_cls = "title--small" -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%} The hour-axis column is a fixed col--span-1 against each day column's
col--span-4 — with the framework's grid auto-sizing tracks from the SUM of spans actually
present in the row (repeat(auto-fit, minmax(0,1fr)); confirmed against the compiled CSS, not
a fixed 12-column grid), the axis's WIDTH SHARE is 1/(1 + 4*days.size): a fine ~7.7% at the
3-day baseline this was tuned against, but a wildly disproportionate 20% at 1 day (noticeable
on any device, worse the bigger/wider the panel — reported on TRMNL X). Scale each day
column's own span up as days.size drops below 3 so the total stays pinned at 13 (1 + 4*3, the
same baseline ratio) instead of shrinking with it — day_span stays the plain 4 at 3+ days,
where the axis was already a sane fraction and unchanged. {%- endcomment -%}
{%- if days.size == 1 -%}
  {%- assign day_span = 12 -%}
{%- elsif days.size == 2 -%}
  {%- assign day_span = 6 -%}
{%- else -%}
  {%- assign day_span = 4 -%}
{%- endif -%}

<div class="layout layout--col gap--none" id="cal-root">
  {% if error and has_events == false %}
    <div class="item">
      <div class="content">
        <span class="title title--small">{{ unavailable_label }}</span>
        <span class="description" data-clamp="2">{{ error }}</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  {% else %}
    <!-- Native framework grid, not an image: percentages (h--[Ncqh]) are computed
         server-side in transform.py as a share of the whole screen, so this fills any
         device — including the larger, portrait, 4-bit TRMNL X — via the framework's
         own container-query system instead of a fixed-aspect picture. -->

    <!-- Header gets its own visual "zone" — solid black, so it reads as a deliberate header
         bar instead of day labels just floating on the same white as the grid below with
         nothing marking where one ends and the other begins. Day label only now — the
         weather icon/high-low that used to share this bar as a second line moved to its own
         footer zone below the grid instead (see FOOTER_PCT in transform.js and the footer
         block further down). header_pct itself stays the same size regardless (see HEADER_PCT
         in transform.js for why: half_horizontal's own title--large tier already needs the
         full amount just for one line, pill padding or not) — the footer's cost comes out of
         gridPct instead. flex-shrink:0/flex-grow:0 + a duplicated inline height alongside the
         h--[Ncqh] class: same defensive fix as the all-day row below (see its comment) — this
         is also a flex child of #cal-root's flex--col, so it's exposed to the identical class
         of bug even though it wasn't the one actually reported shrinking. -->
    <div class="grid gap--none h--[{{ header_pct }}cqh] bg--black" style="flex-shrink:0;flex-grow:0;height:{{ header_pct }}cqh;min-height:{{ header_pct }}cqh;">
      <div class="col--span-1"></div>
      {% for day in days %}
        {% assign dlabel = day.label_short %}
        <div class="col--span-{{ day_span }} flex flex--col flex--center-x flex--center-y border--v-1">
          <!-- min-width:0 undoes the flex default of min-width:auto (natural content width),
               which otherwise silently defeats overflow/ellipsis truncation on the child —
               needed at 5-7 day columns, or in quadrant's narrower portrait slot, where the
               label can genuinely run out of room. color:white inline, not a text--white
               class: .title sets its own explicit color (not currentColor), so it wouldn't
               pick up an ancestor's text--white by inheritance — same class of gotcha as
               border--h-1's fixed color noted elsewhere in this file. No padding class on
               the span itself (there used to be one, sized for the old today-pill's
               background) — now that there's no pill, that padding just added dead space
               on top of the font's own line-height, tall enough to blow past header_pct's
               box in the shorter views (half_horizontal/quadrant) and get vertically
               clipped; the wrapper div's own small px--[10px] py--[2px] is enough breathing
               room against the header's edges. -->
          <div class="rounded--small px--[10px] py--[2px] flex flex--row flex--center-y" style="max-width:100%;min-width:0;overflow:hidden;gap:4px;">
            <span class="title {{ head_title_cls }}{% if view != 4 and view != 2 and view != 3 %} cal-full-daylabel{% endif %}" style="white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;display:block;color:white;min-width:0;">{{ dlabel }}</span>
            {%- if day.is_today and day.people.size > 0 -%}
              <!-- Who has something on today, right in its own header — a glance answers "is
                   anyone busy today" without reading every chip below. day.people (see run()
                   in transform.js) is already deduped to one badge per declared person; a
                   category icon never ends up here (it has no person attached). Each badge
                   uses that PERSON's own color (b.hue/b.fg — colorClass/foregroundFor'd in
                   run(), falling back to plain black/white for a person with no color set)
                   rather than one shared black box for everyone, so the header strip itself
                   hints at who before you even reach their initial. currentColor border keeps
                   a visible ring around every badge regardless of its own fill, including
                   against this row's own bg--black header a lighter fill would otherwise sit
                   flush against.

                   Split explicitly into 2 rows here (first ceil(n/2) people, then the rest)
                   rather than relying on a flex-wrap:wrap trick to do it implicitly — however
                   many people have something today, they land in exactly 2 rows instead of one
                   ever-widening row that would either crush the date label or run off the
                   header entirely. head_badge_d (see "main"), not badge_d: sized for its own
                   2-row header strip, not an event chip's solo/paired rail. -->
              {%- assign head_row1_n = day.people.size | plus: 1 | divided_by: 2 -%}
              {%- assign head_row1 = day.people | slice: 0, head_row1_n -%}
              {%- assign head_row2 = day.people | slice: head_row1_n, day.people.size -%}
              <div class="flex" style="flex-direction:column;gap:1px;flex-shrink:0;">
                <div class="flex" style="flex-direction:row;gap:1px;">
                  {%- for b in head_row1 -%}
                    <span class="rounded--small flex flex--center-x flex--center-y text--{{ b.fg }} bg--{{ b.hue }}" style="width:{{ head_badge_d }}px;height:{{ head_badge_d }}px;flex-shrink:0;overflow:hidden;border:1.5px solid currentColor;box-sizing:border-box;">
                      {%- render "badge_content", b: b -%}
                    </span>
                  {%- endfor -%}
                </div>
                {%- if head_row2.size > 0 -%}
                  <div class="flex" style="flex-direction:row;gap:1px;">
                    {%- for b in head_row2 -%}
                      <span class="rounded--small flex flex--center-x flex--center-y text--{{ b.fg }} bg--{{ b.hue }}" style="width:{{ head_badge_d }}px;height:{{ head_badge_d }}px;flex-shrink:0;overflow:hidden;border:1.5px solid currentColor;box-sizing:border-box;">
                        {%- render "badge_content", b: b -%}
                      </span>
                    {%- endfor -%}
                  </div>
                {%- endif -%}
              </div>
            {%- endif -%}
          </div>
        </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>

    {% if allday_pct > 0 %}
      <div class="grid gap--none h--[{{ allday_pct }}cqh]" style="flex-shrink:0;flex-grow:0;height:{{ allday_pct }}cqh;min-height:{{ allday_pct }}cqh;">
        <!-- Gutter label for the all-day band, same column the hour axis sits in below it —
             without this the gutter here was just blank, giving no hint what the colored bars
             above the hour grid actually are. Deliberately its own small fixed size (label--
             small) rather than axis_cls (the hour numbers' own per-view tier): axis_cls can run
             up to label--xxlarge on the full view, sized for a 1-2 digit number, not a word —
             translated via all_day_label (see allDayText in transform.js), so it's kept short
             in every language on purpose. Left un-clamped (free to wrap to two lines) rather
             than truncated: the row is tall enough now (ALLDAY_ROW_PCT) that two short lines
             fit comfortably, and a wrapped real word reads better than an ellipsis on this one. -->
        <div class="col--span-1 flex flex--col flex--center-x flex--center-y" style="overflow:hidden;box-sizing:border-box;padding:0 2px;text-align:center;">
          <span class="label label--small">{{ all_day_label }}</span>
        </div>
        {% for day in days %}
          <div class="col--span-{{ day_span }} flex flex--col gap--xsmall px--[4px] border--v-1">
            {% for a in day.allday %}
              <!-- A multi-day all-day event used to repeat as an identical, fully-rounded
                   pill in every day column it touched, which read as several unrelated
                   events instead of one continuous span. Square off whichever edge is
                   mid-span (continues_before/after, computed in transform.py from whether
                   the event's real start/end falls outside this specific day) and pull that
                   edge out past the column's own px--[4px] inset with a matching negative
                   margin, so the two neighboring pieces sit flush against the day divider
                   instead of leaving a gap, reading as one bar crossing the boundary. -->
              {% if a.continues_before and a.continues_after %}
                {% assign ad_radius = "0px" %}
              {% elsif a.continues_after %}
                {% assign ad_radius = "7px 0 0 7px" %}
              {% elsif a.continues_before %}
                {% assign ad_radius = "0 7px 7px 0" %}
              {% else %}
                {% assign ad_radius = "7px" %}
              {% endif %}
              <!-- Whichever edge is mid-span gets no outline at all there (see ad_radius just
                   above — same continues_before/after check), so the seam ad_radius/the
                   negative margins already hide doesn't get reintroduced by a border crossing
                   it. Fed into the shared "chip" template as border_left/border_right. -->
              {% assign allday_border_left = true %}
              {% if a.continues_before %}{% assign allday_border_left = false %}{% endif %}
              {% assign allday_border_right = true %}
              {% if a.continues_after %}{% assign allday_border_right = false %}{% endif %}
              <!-- size_class/size_style: how THIS chip type gets its height — see the "chip"
                   template's own comment for why that has to stay a real parameter (an all-day
                   row is normal-flow and needs an explicit height; a timed event is sized by
                   its absolutely-positioned ancestor instead). -->
              {% capture allday_size_class %}h--[{{ allday_row_pct }}cqh]{% endcapture %}
              {% capture allday_size_style %}height:{{ allday_row_pct }}cqh;min-height:{{ allday_row_pct }}cqh;flex-shrink:0;flex-grow:0;{% endcapture %}
              <!-- a.hue/a.fg are computed server-side (see colorClass/foregroundFor in
                   transform.js): a.hue is already the full bg-- suffix (a chromatic hue's
                   fixed pastel step, e.g. "pink-65", or an explicit "gray-N" pin), and a.fg
                   is the matching black/white foreground — a gray shade spans near-black to
                   near-white so it genuinely needs a per-shade contrast decision, unlike the
                   hues (all fixed at one step light enough for black text uniformly). -->
              <!-- w--full: the framework's .flex--col sets align-items:center (not the
                   usual stretch), so without this every chip shrink-wraps to its own title's
                   text width and centers in the column instead of spanning it. Left/right
                   breathing room is handled entirely on the title span itself now (padding-
                   left varies with whether a rail is present, see below; padding-right is a
                   fixed 8px) rather than on this container, since the rail is an absolutely-
                   positioned overlay, not a flex sibling the container's own padding would
                   need to make room for. -->
              <!-- Outline in currentColor (= a.fg, via the text--{{ a.fg }} class added here
                   too, not just on the title span below, so the border actually inherits it —
                   a chip's own background can otherwise sit flush against an equally-light/
                   dark day-background segment with no visible edge at all, e.g. a light
                   gray-N pin over today's unshaded (white) daytime band. Suppressed on
                   whichever side is mid-span for a continuing multi-day banner (see
                   continues_before/after above) so the outline doesn't reintroduce the seam
                   between fragments that ad_radius/the negative margins above already work to
                   hide — only the banner's real start/end edges, and always its top/bottom,
                   get one.

                   h--[{{ allday_row_pct }}cqh]: every all-day row is exactly this height,
                   period — not content-driven. Without an explicit height here, this box has
                   no real vertical budget for cal-fit-text/cal-fit-clip to measure against
                   (a content-driven box's clientHeight just grows to match whatever content
                   is already there, so the "does it fit?" check downstream in fit() never
                   actually fails) — the title would silently pick the largest allowed font
                   regardless of how little text it has, and different days' chips would end
                   up visibly different heights depending on which one happened to render
                   first. A fixed height fixes both: a real ceiling fit() can shrink against,
                   and a genuine minimum every row gets regardless of content.

                   ROOT-CAUSE HISTORY, kept because the symptom is easy to reintroduce the same
                   way twice: this row previously rendered as h--[cqh] — the allday_row_pct
                   merge variable resolving to EMPTY, not the height going missing after some
                   later reflow. Every view file's render-main call (see full.liquid etc.) passes
                   a fixed, explicit list of variables into the template block below (Liquid's
                   render tag does NOT auto-inherit the caller's scope, unlike include) —
                   allday_row_pct had
                   simply never been added to that list when it was introduced, so it read as
                   nil here regardless of what transform.js/​.trmnlp.yml set it to. An empty
                   bracket value means no real height rule at all, so the box fell back to
                   content-driven auto-height — exactly the "no real vertical budget" failure
                   mode described above, which let fit()'s binary search lock in its max font
                   size (since an auto-height box always "fits" whatever's tried). Severity
                   scaled with column width: wide views mostly hid it (a big font still fit on
                   one or two lines), narrow ones (quadrant, half_vertical) wrapped that oversize
                   font across many lines and inflated the row to hundreds of pixels tall. Fixed
                   by adding allday_row_pct to the render call in every *.liquid view file.
                   flex-shrink:0/flex-grow:0 and the duplicated inline height below are kept as
                   cheap extra insurance (a fixed-size row should never be flex-resized by its
                   container regardless of cause) but were not themselves the fix. -->
              <!-- rail_radius mirrors ad_radius's LEFT side only — the rail sits flush against
                   the chip's own left edge, so it needs to be square exactly when the chip's
                   own left edge is (continues_before, a mid-span multi-day fragment), and
                   rounded to match otherwise. Passed into the shared "chip" template below
                   rather than computed there since a timed event (see its own call further
                   down) has no "which corner is mid-span" question to answer — only an all-day
                   chip can continue across a day boundary. -->
              {% if a.continues_before %}
                {% assign this_rail_radius = "0" %}
              {% else %}
                {% assign this_rail_radius = "7px 0 0 7px" %}
              {% endif %}
              <!-- The whole chip — fill/border/radius/badges/title — is one render of
                   the shared "chip" template (see its own definition above for the full
                   parameter rundown and why: this used to be its own hand-written <div>, which
                   is exactly how the all-day accent bar (bar:, see transform.js) ended up
                   missing entirely for a while — two copies of "the same" markup drift apart
                   silently. size_class/size_style give this row its real height (see the
                   h--[Ncqh] comment above); fit_min=9/clip=true match that: unlike a timed
                   event's absolutely-positioned overlay, an oversized title here needs
                   cal-fit-clip to keep it from growing the row past its allday_pct allocation. -->
              {% render "chip", hue: a.hue, fg: a.fg, bar: a.bar, title: a.title, badges: a.badges, display: a.display, flex_dir: "flex--row", size_class: allday_size_class, size_style: allday_size_style, pad_cls: "py--[1px]", box_radius: ad_radius, border_left: allday_border_left, border_right: allday_border_right, margin_left: a.continues_before, margin_right: a.continues_after, title_cls: chip_title_cls, fit_min: 9, clip: true, pad_right: pad_right, rail_radius: this_rail_radius, bar_w: bar_w, badge_d: badge_d, image_d: image_d %}
            {% endfor %}
          </div>
        {% endfor %}
      </div>
    {% endif %}

    <div class="grid gap--none h--[{{ grid_pct }}cqh]" style="flex-shrink:0;flex-grow:0;height:{{ grid_pct }}cqh;min-height:{{ grid_pct }}cqh;">
      <div class="col--span-1 flex flex--col gap--none">
        {% for row in hour_rows %}
          <!-- Zebra-stripe by hour instead of drawing boundary lines: five different
               thin-line techniques all verified correct server-side and rendered fine
               locally, but never once showed up on the actual device. A per-hour
               background shade is a large-area fill instead of a 1-2px sliver, so it
               can't fail the same way. -->
          <div class="h--[{{ row.pct }}cqh] w--full flex flex--row flex--center-x cal-row cal-fit-box {% if row.shade == 1 %}bg--gray-65{% endif %}">
            <!-- Row height varies a lot with the configured hour range and view size — the
                 emphasized (day_start-day_end-derived) hours get much taller rows than the
                 compressed hours outside it, and that ratio shifts per view too. A fixed
                 per-view font size clipped on some configurations, so this reuses the same
                 fit-text sizing as event titles instead of hand-tuning one that only works
                 for one row-height assumption. Even fit down to its floor, an hour number can
                 still be taller than a truly tiny compressed row (verified: quadrant's most
                 compressed rows are ~4px, shorter than even an 8px minimum font) — the box's
                 own overflow:hidden (see script below) just clips those specific numbers,
                 which is fine: the emphasized hours (the ones actually worth reading) fit
                 exactly, and used to be hidden on quadrant entirely regardless of size. -->
              <span class="label {{ axis_cls }} {% if row.bold %}font--bold{% endif %} cal-fit-text cal-axis-text{% if row.important %} cal-axis-important{% endif %}" data-fit-min="8" data-fit-max="40">{{ row.hour }}</span>
          </div>
        {% endfor %}
      </div>
      {% for day in days %}
        <div class="col--span-{{ day_span }} flex flex--col gap--none border--v-1" style="position:relative;">
          <!-- Background bands always render at full per-hour granularity (see
               _layout_native) even where an event is running — events are a separate
               absolutely-positioned overlay (below), painted on top, instead of the
               background flattening into one shade for the event's whole span. That used
               to make a multi-hour event show a single flat color behind it where the
               surrounding spacers were still alternating. -->
          {% for seg in day.segments %}
            {% if seg.night %}
              <!-- Sunrise/sunset used to draw a thin tinted line (like "now"), but on the
                   real grayscale device a 1-3px sliver against an already-striped background
                   was unreadable. Shading the whole night span is a large-area signal instead
                   — same fix as the hour zebra-stripe, applied to day/night. Solid black read
                   as too harsh, and so — per direct feedback — did the first pass of grays
                   picked here (gray-20/30 for night, gray-70/40 for non-today daytime): a
                   large-area fill at those darker/higher-contrast steps still reads as heavy
                   across a whole column of hour cells, not just a thin line. Lightened the
                   whole scale up a couple of steps and narrowed the shaded/unshaded gap
                   (gray-N: lower number is darker) while keeping the same relative
                   ordering — night still clearly darker than any daytime tone, today's
                   shading still a touch lighter than a non-today day's. -->
              {% assign seg_bg = "bg--gray-30" %}
              {% if seg.shade == 1 %}{% assign seg_bg = "bg--gray-40" %}{% endif %}
            {% elsif day.is_today and seg.past %}
              <!-- An hour today that's already passed reads as "done," same as any other
                   already-happened day — so it gets the exact same treatment as a non-today
                   day below, instead of staying in today's brighter scheme forever. Only
                   upcoming hours (seg.past false) keep today's own lighter look, so the
                   grid visually "fills in" as the day goes on. seg.past is only ever set on
                   today's column (see layoutNative) — every other day always falls straight
                   through to the plain "is today, still upcoming" branch below. -->
              {% assign seg_bg = "bg--gray-70" %}
              {% if seg.shade == 1 %}{% assign seg_bg = "bg--gray-55" %}{% endif %}
            {% elsif day.is_today %}
              {% assign seg_bg = "" %}
              {% if seg.shade == 1 %}{% assign seg_bg = "bg--gray-65" %}{% endif %}
            {% else %}
              {% assign seg_bg = "bg--gray-70" %}
              {% if seg.shade == 1 %}{% assign seg_bg = "bg--gray-55" %}{% endif %}
            {% endif %}
            <div class="h--[{{ seg.pct }}cqh] w--full cal-row {{ seg_bg }}" style="position:relative;">
              {% if seg.weather %}
                <!-- Significant weather (rain/snow/storm/fog) as a diagonal hatch instead of
                     an icon or line — a repeating pattern is still a large-area signal (survives
                     the same way the zebra-stripe does) but reads as "texture" layered on top
                     of the night/day fill rather than replacing it, so day/night stays visible
                     underneath. One visual treatment for all categories for now; distinguishing
                     rain vs snow vs storm visually can follow if that turns out to matter. -->
                <div style="position:absolute;top:0;left:0;right:0;bottom:0;background-image:repeating-linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(0,0,0,0.4) 0px, rgba(0,0,0,0.4) 3px, transparent 3px, transparent 9px);"></div>
              {% endif %}
            </div>
          {% endfor %}
          {% if day.now_marker %}
            <!-- Absolutely positioned like events, independent of the background segment
                 list — it used to be spliced INTO a segment and enforced to a minimum
                 visible height by borrowing pct from whichever other segment in today's
                 column happened to be largest. That shrank a same-hour band in today's
                 column relative to every other column (none of which ever borrow, since
                 none of them have a "now" mark), so today's zebra stripe stopped lining up
                 with the rest — the same class of bug the event-overlay refactor fixed. -->
            <div style="position:absolute;left:0;right:0;top:{{ day.now_marker.top_pct }}%;">
              {% if day.now_marker.night %}
                <div class="h--[5px] w--full bg--white"></div>
              {% else %}
                <div class="h--[5px] w--full bg--red"></div>
              {% endif %}
            </div>
          {% endif %}
          {% for event in day.events %}
            <!-- Absolutely positioned over the background stack above, sized in plain CSS %
                 (relative to this day column, which is the nearest position:relative
                 ancestor) rather than the cqh unit used elsewhere — cqh is relative to the
                 whole .layout, not this nested column, so it would double-count the header/
                 all-day rows' own height if used here.

                 Each overlapping event is its OWN independently-positioned box now — top_pct/
                 height_pct are THIS event's own actual start/end (not a shared cluster span
                 all its lanes used to stretch to fill, which used to render a short event as
                 tall as a much longer one it merely overlapped — confirmed against real
                 data). lane_index/nlanes place it in its horizontal slot among however many
                 events actually overlap it at that moment; the small side padding below
                 (box-sizing:border-box, so it insets rather than growing the slot) stands in
                 for the gap the old grid--cols layout got for free between lanes. -->
            <div style="position:absolute;top:{{ event.top_pct }}%;height:{{ event.height_pct }}%;left:calc({{ event.lane_index }} * 100% / {{ event.nlanes }});width:calc(100% / {{ event.nlanes }});box-sizing:border-box;padding:0 2px;">
              <!-- event.hue/event.fg are computed server-side (see colorClass/foregroundFor in
                   transform.js) — event.hue is already the full bg-- suffix, event.fg the
                   matching foreground. text--{{ event.fg }} is added on this outer div (not
                   just the title span below) so the currentColor border actually inherits it
                   — gives the chip a visible edge even against an equally light/dark
                   background band it would otherwise sit flush against with no boundary at
                   all. box-sizing:border-box keeps the border from growing the box past its
                   own h--full/w--full allocation.
                   NOT cal-row: that class also carries a blanket JS-forced overflow:hidden
                   (see run() below, needed for the axis/background rows whose size actually
                   is content-driven) which would re-clip a too-tall title right back after
                   fit() just set this event's own cal-fit-box to overflow:visible below. -->
              <!-- The whole chip — fill/border/radius/badges/title — is the SAME render
                   of the shared "chip" template the all-day chip above uses (see its own
                   definition for the full parameter rundown). size_class "h--full w--full"
                   instead of an explicit height: this box is sized entirely by its own
                   absolutely-positioned ancestor (top_pct/height_pct just above), not by a
                   normal-flow row budget the way an all-day chip is — that's also why
                   fit_min=11/clip=false here (vs. the all-day call's 9/true): fit() can safely
                   leave an oversized title visible-overflow on this overlay instead of needing
                   cal-fit-clip to protect a shared row height. box_radius "7px" (matching
                   rounded--small's real compiled value) and border_left/border_right both true
                   unconditionally: a timed event never spans across day columns, so it has no
                   "which corner is mid-span" question to answer the way an all-day chip does. -->
              {% render "chip", hue: event.hue, fg: event.fg, bar: event.bar, title: event.title, badges: event.badges, display: event.display, flex_dir: "flex--col", size_class: "h--full w--full", size_style: "", pad_cls: event_pad_cls, box_radius: "7px", border_left: true, border_right: true, margin_left: false, margin_right: false, title_cls: event_title_cls, fit_min: 11, clip: false, pad_right: pad_right, rail_radius: timed_rail_radius, bar_w: bar_w, badge_d: badge_d, image_d: image_d %}
            </div>
          {% endfor %}
        </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>

    <!-- Footer: daily weather (icon + high/low), its own black zone below the grid — used
         to be a second line squeezed into the header alongside the day label (see header_pct/
         FOOTER_PCT in transform.js), pulled out here instead so the header stays one line and
         the weather gets a deliberate zone of its own rather than competing for space at the
         top. Same col--span-1 gutter + col--span-{{ day_span }} per-day-column shape as the
         header above, so the icon/temp under each day line up with that day's own column
         exactly. flex-shrink:0/flex-grow:0 + duplicated inline height: same defensive fix as
         every other fixed-size zone in this file (see the all-day row's own comment for why). -->
    <div class="grid gap--none h--[{{ footer_pct }}cqh] bg--black" style="flex-shrink:0;flex-grow:0;height:{{ footer_pct }}cqh;min-height:{{ footer_pct }}cqh;">
      <div class="col--span-1"></div>
      {% for day in days %}
        <div class="col--span-{{ day_span }} flex flex--row flex--center-x flex--center-y gap--xsmall border--v-1" style="overflow:hidden;">
          {% if day.icon %}
            <!-- height:1.6em ties the icon to whatever chip_title_cls's font-size resolves
                 to (so it still grows/shrinks across view tiers) while rendering noticeably
                 larger than the text itself. filter:invert(1): plain black line art (same
                 SVGs used elsewhere against a light background) would otherwise vanish
                 against this footer's black fill. -->
            <img class="{{ chip_title_cls }}" style="height:1.6em;width:auto;flex-shrink:0;filter:invert(1);" src="{{ day.icon }}">
            <!-- color:white inline, not a text--white class: .label sets its own explicit
                 color (not currentColor), so it wouldn't pick up an ancestor's text--white
                 by inheritance — same gotcha as .title on the day label above. -->
            <span class="label {{ chip_title_cls }}" style="white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;min-width:0;color:white;">{{ day.temp.high }}° / {{ day.temp.low }}°</span>
          {% endif %}
        </div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  {% endif %}
</div>

<script>
(function () {
  // Binary-search the largest font size that fits each event title inside its own
  // block, measured against the real rendered box (adapted from the fit-text technique
  // in trmnl-moppen-plugin) — more robust than guessing a size tier server-side, since
  // it reacts to the block's *actual* pixel height on whichever device renders it.
  // Returns the font size (px) it settled on, or null if there was no box to fit against.
  // Pass `forced` to skip the search entirely and just apply that exact size (used to make
  // a whole group of elements — the hour axis — share one uniform size; see run()).
  function fit(el, forced) {
    var box = el.closest('.cal-fit-box');
    if (!box) return null;
    // The axis still needs to clip — an hour number too tall for its row would otherwise
    // auto-expand a flex/grid child and push every later hour off-screen (the box's size
    // there IS content-driven). Same for anything else marked cal-fit-clip: a normal-flow
    // box (like the all-day chip title) that contributes real height to its row's own
    // budget, where letting it grow would push that row taller than its allday_pct
    // allocation and misalign the rest of the grid — not the "harmless" kind of spill event
    // titles get away with. Event titles DON'T have that risk: their box is an absolutely-
    // positioned overlay with a fixed top/height percentage regardless of content, so
    // letting an unavoidably-too-small title spill past its pill (matching how TRMNL's own
    // official calendar plugin handles this — fixed font size, overflow: visible, see
    // github.com/usetrmnl/plugins lib/calendar) reads far better than either clipping
    // mid-glyph or hiding the title outright.
    var mustClip = el.classList.contains('cal-axis-text') || el.classList.contains('cal-fit-clip');
    box.style.overflow = mustClip ? 'hidden' : 'visible';
    el.style.overflowWrap = 'anywhere'; // let a single long word break rather than
                                         // overflow a narrow (multi-lane) block sideways
    el.style.display = 'block';         // make width/maxWidth apply predictably
    var min = parseInt(el.getAttribute('data-fit-min'), 10) || 11;
    var max = parseInt(el.getAttribute('data-fit-max'), 10) || 90;

    function setSize(px) {
      el.style.fontSize = px + 'px';
      el.style.lineHeight = Math.round(px * 1.15) + 'px';
    }

    if (forced != null) {
      setSize(forced);
      return forced;
    }

    // clientHeight/clientWidth include the box's own padding, which the text can't
    // actually occupy — read the real computed padding instead of guessing a fixed
    // buffer (a wrong guess here previously let text render past the edge uncaught).
    var boxCs = getComputedStyle(box);
    var padX = (parseFloat(boxCs.paddingLeft) || 0) + (parseFloat(boxCs.paddingRight) || 0);
    var availW = box.clientWidth - padX - 2;
    if (availW <= 0) { setSize(min); return min; }
    // Give the element its own hard width ceiling and compare it against ITSELF
    // (scrollWidth vs its own clientWidth) rather than against a separate ancestor's
    // measurements — comparing across two different elements' box models proved
    // unreliable here (text rendered clipped despite scrollWidth appearing to fit the
    // parent). Self-comparison after pinning width is a much harder guarantee.
    el.style.maxWidth = availW + 'px';

    function fits() {
      return box.scrollHeight <= box.clientHeight + 1 && el.scrollWidth <= el.clientWidth + 1;
    }

    setSize(max);
    if (fits()) return max;

    var lo = min, hi = max;
    setSize(lo);
    if (!fits()) return lo; // even the minimum doesn't fit — leave it, clipped by the box

    while (hi - lo > 1) {
      var mid = Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);
      setSize(mid);
      if (fits()) { lo = mid; } else { hi = mid; }
    }
    setSize(lo);
    // Binary search assumes fit is monotonic in font size, but wrapping can make a
    // slightly larger size measure as "fits" (text reflows to fewer/narrower lines)
    // right next to a smaller size that doesn't — a non-monotonic edge case the search
    // can land on wrong. Belt-and-braces: step down further, actually re-checking after
    // each step, until it genuinely fits (or we hit the floor).
    while (!fits() && lo > min) {
      lo -= 1;
      setSize(lo);
    }

    // The binary search maximizes font size, which happily picks a size that wraps a
    // single word to a near-empty second line (e.g. "Speelpun" / "t") if that barely
    // satisfies the height budget — technically "fits", but reads as broken. If the
    // chosen size doesn't fit on one line, look for a modestly smaller size that does;
    // prefer a slightly smaller, cleanly single-line title over a bigger, badly-split one.
    var lineH = Math.round(lo * 1.15);
    if (el.scrollHeight > lineH + 2) {
      var found = null;
      var floor = Math.max(min, Math.floor(lo * 0.7));
      for (var s = lo - 1; s >= floor; s--) {
        setSize(s);
        if (el.scrollWidth <= el.clientWidth + 1 && el.scrollHeight <= Math.round(s * 1.15) + 2) {
          found = s;
          break;
        }
      }
      lo = found !== null ? found : lo;
      setSize(lo);
    }
    return lo;
  }

  function run() {
    var root = document.getElementById('cal-root');
    if (!root) return;
    // Every h--[Ncqh] row must be prevented from growing past its allocation — a flex/grid
    // child otherwise auto-expands to fit oversized content (e.g. an hour-number label too
    // tall for a very short row on the small quadrant view), which cascades into the whole
    // grid overflowing and later hours being pushed off-screen. Constrain first, then fit.
    var rows = root.querySelectorAll('.cal-row');
    for (var r = 0; r < rows.length; r++) rows[r].style.overflow = 'hidden';

    // Rotated to portrait, the full-view "OG" device has much taller hour rows to spend
    // (same total pixels, redistributed from width to height) — raise the fit-text ceiling
    // for events and the hour axis there so they can actually use that extra room instead of
    // stopping at the landscape-tuned cap. Only raises the CEILING the binary search may
    // reach; it still only picks a bigger size if the real box can actually fit it.
    var lgPortrait = !!document.querySelector('.screen--lg.screen--portrait');

    // Event titles each fit independently — different lengths, different lane widths.
    var titles = root.querySelectorAll('.cal-fit-text:not(.cal-axis-text)');
    for (var i = 0; i < titles.length; i++) {
      if (lgPortrait) titles[i].setAttribute('data-fit-max', '100');
      fit(titles[i]);
    }

    // Hour axis numbers share ONE size within their own group instead of each fitting
    // independently: "10"/"23" are wider than "0"-"9", so fitting each row independently
    // to its own row's width left two-digit hours visibly smaller than the one-digit hours
    // right next to them, even though they're the same logical label. Fit each once to find
    // what it could individually be, then force every one in the group down to the smallest
    // of those ceilings for a uniform look.
    //
    // Two SEPARATE groups (important-range hours vs. the compressed hours outside it)
    // rather than one group across all 24 — the compressed hours can be a sliver of the
    // important hours' height (that's the whole point of the important-range emphasis), so
    // one shared minimum across all 24 let a single extremely thin compressed row (which
    // can't legibly fit any size and clips regardless) tank the readable important-range
    // labels down to the same tiny floor. Fitting the two zones separately keeps the
    // important hours at the largest size THEY can uniformly support.
    function fitAxisGroup(selector, maxOverride) {
      var els = root.querySelectorAll(selector);
      var minSize = null;
      for (var j = 0; j < els.length; j++) {
        if (maxOverride) els[j].setAttribute('data-fit-max', maxOverride);
        var s = fit(els[j]);
        if (s != null && (minSize === null || s < minSize)) minSize = s;
      }
      if (minSize !== null) {
        for (var k = 0; k < els.length; k++) fit(els[k], minSize);
      }
    }
    fitAxisGroup('.cal-axis-important', lgPortrait ? '48' : null);
    fitAxisGroup('.cal-axis-text:not(.cal-axis-important)', lgPortrait ? '48' : null);
  }

  // Wait for the Inter webfont to finish loading before measuring. If fit() measures
  // against the fallback font (narrower/wider metrics) before Inter swaps in, the text
  // reflows after fit() has already locked in a size, silently overflowing the box it
  // was just fitted to (clipped by the box's own overflow:hidden with no way to react).
  //
  // BUT document.fonts.ready can take a while (or, on a render sandbox with restricted/
  // slow font fetches, might not resolve before the render pipeline captures its screenshot
  // at all) — and never running fit() at all is far worse than running it once against the
  // fallback font: text is left completely unclamped, overflowing small event boxes badly
  // instead of just reading at slightly-off metrics. Race a hard timeout against fonts.ready
  // so fit() always runs at least once quickly, then again (harmless — it's idempotent) once
  // fonts.ready actually resolves, correcting for the real font's metrics if it arrived late.
  function runAfterFonts() {
    setTimeout(run, 400);
    if (document.fonts && document.fonts.ready) {
      document.fonts.ready.then(run).catch(run);
    } else {
      run();
    }
  }

  if (document.readyState === 'loading') {
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', runAfterFonts);
  } else {
    runAfterFonts();
  }
})();
</script>
{% endtemplate %}
